comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-17T00:41:08.759Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation (same branch as pm-3627y1, pm-ycgb5g and pm-3fbj1f, because the member pass added by pm-3fbj1f walks the same payload): stringLeaves now walks with an explicit stack. childSteps lists an array's elements (skipping holes, as the old flatMap did) or an object's own enumerable entries, and pushReversed pushes them in reverse one at a time, so the first child is walked first and a very wide object never spreads into one call. Leaves keep the old depth-first order, and an object is marked seen when it is walked, as before, so the existing guard specs pass unchanged. scanPayloadMembers uses the same walk with each step carrying its key context. The O(n^2) findIndex dedupe in scanMutationSecrets became a Set keyed by rule and path, with the same first-occurrence order. Measured on Node 26: a 100,000-level { token: ... } chain with a credential at the bottom scans in about 430 ms and evaluateMutationGuard in block mode throws mutation_secret_guard_blocked (before: RangeError near 2,000 levels, then secret_guard_scan_failed_open). A 100,000-key flat object takes about 2.46 s against 2.04 s for the released 2026.11.16 guard; the existing per-leaf token rules dominate, and the member pass adds about 20 percent. The spec pins a 100,000-level array chain (leaf rule) and object chain (member rule), block-mode refusal at that depth, and depth-first finding order across siblings and nesting.\n"
  "2026-11-17T01:18:00.106Z","harness:claude-code","Codex review round 3 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) found a fail-open regression in the first explicit-stack walk. childSteps called the payload array's own forEach, so an array whose forEach property was overridden (Object.assign([], { forEach: null })) threw inside the scan, and block mode returned secret_guard_scan_failed_open instead of blocking a token elsewhere in the payload. The merge base blocked it. childSteps now reads an array through Object.keys filtered to canonical index keys, which never calls a payload-owned method, skips holes, and costs only the present elements of a sparse array with length 2^32 - 1. The spec pins block-mode refusal with forEach, map, flatMap, filter, keys, entries and Symbol.iterator each overridden, a sparse array scanned in well under a second, and non-index array properties ignored as before.\n"
  "2026-11-17T01:49:13.492Z","harness:claude-code","PR 1745 Greptile review (P1), fixed in b4374fe3b: reading arrays through Object.keys skipped non-enumerable elements, which the old flatMap walk and JSON.stringify both read. The digit-only filter also accepted 4294967295, which is not an array index. childSteps now uses Object.getOwnPropertyNames filtered to canonical indices no larger than 2^32 - 2. Payload-owned methods are still never called, and a sparse array still costs only its present elements. The spec pins a non-enumerable element and the out-of-range numeric key.\n"
